WEBVTT

00:00:00.034 --> 00:00:03.020
A productive industrial hunt starts with environment knowledge.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:07.475
Asset inventory should identify systems, locations, owners, functions, criticality,

00:00:07.475 --> 00:00:08.873
and expected communication.

00:00:08.873 --> 00:00:12.965
A network address becomes meaningful when the hunter knows it is an HMI, controller,

00:00:12.965 --> 00:00:16.053
historian, engineering workstation, or remote-access gateway.

00:00:16.053 --> 00:00:20.680
A Purdue-style architecture can organize field, control, supervisory, site-operations,

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.050
industrial-DMZ, and enterprise functions.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.060
It is a reference rather than a perfect map.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.630
Hunters need the real zones, conduits, serial and wireless paths, vendor connections,

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:31.700
and services crossing each boundary.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.900
PLCs and RTUs execute or support control.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.345
HMIs and SCADA platforms provide operator visibility and supervisory authority.

00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:40.120
Historians preserve process trends.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.145
Engineering workstations hold privileged programming tools.

00:00:43.145 --> 00:00:46.670
Remote-access systems connect support personnel to selected assets.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.540
Each role changes the significance of observed activity.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:53.950
Normal behavior includes expected peers, protocols, timing, commands, user roles,

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:54.930
and process modes.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.160
Startup, shutdown, failover, batch transitions, maintenance,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.985
and seasonal operation may all produce different patterns.

00:01:00.985 --> 00:01:04.610
A hunt based on one quiet baseline can misclassify legitimate change.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.030
Asset criticality and dependency shape scope.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:10.700
Unexpected traffic toward a controller may deserve different handling from the same

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.425
traffic toward a reporting server.

00:01:12.425 --> 00:01:16.560
Hunters should know which systems preserve safety, control, visibility, redundancy,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.465
and recovery.

00:01:17.465 --> 00:01:20.560
Operators and engineers supply context that telemetry cannot.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.265
They can validate work orders, explain process faults,

00:01:23.265 --> 00:01:26.795
identify unusual equipment behavior, and advise safe escalation.

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:30.755
Hunters should document assumptions and gaps rather than treating incomplete inventory or

00:01:30.755 --> 00:01:32.080
architecture as certainty.
